      "dom_id": "question-84695",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A processor manufacturer determines that $2%$ of the processors it produces are defective. A quality control officer inspects a random sample of $500$ processors. What is the standard deviation of the number of defective processors found in the sample? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective processors.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=500$ processors\n\n- the probability of a processor being defective is $p=2%=0.02$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(500,0.02).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of defective processors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(500)(0.02)(1-0.02)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9.8} \\\\  & =3.13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
