      "dom_id": "question-130761",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A new drug has a $75%$ probability of curing a patient within a week. If $50$ patients are treated, what is the variance of the number of cured patients within a week?",
      "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug cures a patient within a week is $p=75%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.75).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(1-0.75) \\\\  & =(50)(0.75)(0.25) \\\\  & =9.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
